is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is healthy; there is no failure on the inter-router link. End-to-end validation shows pc1 (195.11.14.2) can reach pc2 (200.1.1.2) with 0% loss in both directions. The interconnect between the border routers (router1:eth0 ↔ router2:eth0) is up at 10Gb/s, full duplex, with “Link detected: yes” on both sides. Control-plane is operational: BGP is Established between router1 (AS 1, neighbor 193.10.11.2) and router2 (AS 2, neighbor 193.10.11.1), with prefixes exchanged (router1 receives 200.1.1.0/24; router2 receives 195.11.14.0/24). Data-plane routes are installed correctly: router1 forwards 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 on eth0; router2 forwards 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1 on eth0. This explains why pings succeed: packets from pc1 traverse router1→router2 and reach pc2 without loss.

Localization: No faulty subsystem found. Interfaces router1:eth0 and router2:eth0 are up; FRR bgpd on both routers is stable (Up/Down ~1–2 minutes at time of check), and routing tables are consistent.

Root cause: No outage is present—reported loss was not reproducible; the inter-router link and BGP are fully operational.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Recommend the user re-test and, if issues recur, capture exact timestamps and test commands; also consider checking for host-side issues (e.g., mistyped IP, transient ARP/DNS) and enable brief monitoring/alerts on BGP and interface status to catch any intermittent events. 
=== END ===